- **Application Load Balancer (ALB)** — The Application Load Balancer (ALB) works at the HTTP layer — layer 7.
- **health checks** — It does TLS termination (your certificate lives on the ALB, not on every server), sticky sessions when you need them, and — critically — health checks: it periodically asks each server "are you alive and well?" and stops sending traffic to any server that fails.
- **Network Load Balancer (NLB)** — The Network Load Balancer (NLB) works at the transport layer — layer 4.
- **listener** — The anatomy to memorize: a listener (port + protocol + certificate) forwards to rules that pick a target group — the actual pool of servers — and the target group runs health checks against a path you define, like /health.
- **health check path** — Three tunables matter: the health check path (make it a cheap endpoint that verifies the app, not just the web server), the healthy/unhealthy thresholds (how many consecutive passes/fails before the verdict changes — flapping servers need hysteresis), and the deregistration de…
- **Auto Scaling group (ASG)** — An Auto Scaling group (ASG) has three ingredients.

