- **Decision 1 — Image.** — Decision 1 — Image. The OS and starting software: Ubuntu, Windows Server, Debian, or a marketplace image with preinstalled stacks.
- **Ubuntu 2204 LTS** — For this track: Ubuntu 2204 LTS for Linux work, Windows Server 2022 Datacenter when Windows is needed.
- **Decision 2 — Size.** — Decision 2 — Size. Azure VM sizes are families: B-series (burstable, cheap, for dev/test and low-traffic — your lab default), D-series (general purpose, the production workhorse), E-series (memory-optimized, for databases), F-series (compute-optimized), N-series (GPU, for ML/r…
- **Decision 3 — Disks.** — For the interview: "B for bursty dev, D for general production, E for memory-heavy, N for GPU." Decision 3 — Disks. Every VM gets an OS disk; add data disks for application data.
- **Standard HDD** — Disk types: Standard HDD (cheapest, for archival), Standard SSD (balanced), Premium SSD (production I/O), Ultra Disk (extreme I/O, region-limited).
- **Decision 4 — Networking.** — Decision 4 — Networking. The VM lands in a virtual network (Module 4's whole topic) — for now, accept the defaults the portal proposes, but note the public IP and NSG choices, because "VM with a public IP and an open SSH port to the world" is how lab VMs get cryptomined.

