- **Computer anatomy** — CPU executes, RAM holds working data, storage persists it; the OS kernel schedules processes and guards hardware.
- **The internet stack** — DNS turns names into IPs, TCP/IP moves packets reliably, HTTP is the application conversation on top.
- **Packet journey** — browser → DNS lookup → TCP handshake → HTTP request → server → response; every hop is observable with the right tool.
- **Addressing** — IPv4 addresses plus subnet masks carve networks; private ranges (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16) never route on the public internet.
- **Binary counting** — /24 = 256 addresses, /28 = 16; the mask's 1-bits are the network, 0-bits are hosts.

