A graph has vertices V and edges E (unordered pairs). Degree deg(v) = incident edge count. Handshaking lemma: Σdeg(v) = 2|E| — every edge contributes 2 to the degree sum. Corollary: the number of odd-degree vertices is even. Worked example (verified): a network with 5 routers and 6 links. Degrees: A:3, B:2, C:3, D:2, E:2. Sum = 3+2+3+2+2 = 12 = 2×6 ✓. Odd-degree vertices: A, C (two of them — even count ✓). Eulerian trail (use every edge once) exists iff 0 or 2 vertices have odd degree — this graph qualifies (A…C trail). Network topology, dependency graphs, and state machines are graphs; the lemma is a 10-second sanity check on any degree data ("sum is odd ⇒ your data is wrong").

